WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.311
Scenario 1 walkthrough: Customer Support Resolution Agent.

2
00:00:04.311 --> 00:00:15.284
A support agent that can issue refunds is where prompt-only rules fail in production, so this scenario keeps asking whether a rule belongs in a prompt, a hook or code.

3
00:00:16.684 --> 00:00:20.946
Scenario one is a customer support agent built on the Agent SDK.

4
00:00:20.946 --> 00:00:32.867
It talks to customers about returns, billing disputes and account problems, and it acts through MCP tools such as get customer, lookup order, process refund and escalate to human.

5
00:00:32.867 --> 00:00:39.127
The business wants most issues solved in one contact, without the agent doing anything unsafe.

6
00:00:40.527 --> 00:00:43.436
Expect questions from several task statements.

7
00:00:43.436 --> 00:00:46.788
The loop and enforced workflow steps from domain one.

8
00:00:46.788 --> 00:00:48.812
Hooks that intercept tool calls.

9
00:00:48.812 --> 00:00:52.923
Tool descriptions and structured error responses from domain two.

10
00:00:52.923 --> 00:01:00.260
And from domain five, keeping exact case facts across a long conversation, and knowing when to hand over to a human.

11
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:02.897
A classic decision.

12
00:01:02.897 --> 00:01:08.303
The agent occasionally issues a refund before it has verified who it is talking to.

13
00:01:08.303 --> 00:01:12.602
Making the prompt more emphatic lowers the rate but never to zero.

14
00:01:12.602 --> 00:01:21.329
The exam wants a deterministic gate, so the refund tool is blocked by code or a pre tool use hook until verification has actually run.

15
00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:25.262
Now a contrast where the answer changes.

16
00:01:25.262 --> 00:01:28.808
If the customer asks for a human, honour it immediately.

17
00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:30.708
If policy has a gap, escalate.

18
00:01:30.708 --> 00:01:38.117
But frustration alone is not a reason when the agent can fix the problem, and self-rated confidence is a poor signal.

19
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:42.676
When you do hand off, send a structured summary, not the raw transcript.

20
00:01:44.076 --> 00:01:45.860
Watch for these distractors.

21
00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.129
Prompt-only limits on refunds, where a hook should enforce the cap.

22
00:01:50.129 --> 00:01:53.314
Escalating on sentiment instead of clear criteria.

23
00:01:53.314 --> 00:01:57.137
Vague error messages that leave the agent unable to recover.

24
00:01:57.137 --> 00:02:02.871
And guessing between several matching customers rather than asking for another identifier.

25
00:02:04.271 --> 00:02:06.833
For scenario one, remember four things.

26
00:02:06.833 --> 00:02:11.102
Required ordering and money limits are enforced in code or hooks.

27
00:02:11.102 --> 00:02:15.898
Escalation follows explicit criteria and comes with a structured handoff.

28
00:02:15.898 --> 00:02:20.890
Tool errors say what kind of failure it was and whether a retry makes sense.

29
00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:26.671
And exact amounts and identifiers live in a case facts block that summaries cannot blur.

